It is an [[evergreen]] [[vine|climber]] growing to {{convert|3|-|6|m|ft|0|abbr=on}}, with heart-shaped glossy leaves to {{convert|30|cm|0|abbr=on}} long, and occasionally [[bract|spathes]] of white flowers in mature plants. With a minimum temperature requirement of {{convert|15|C|F|abbr=on}}, in [[temperateness|temperate]] regions it must be grown under glass or as a [[houseplant]].<ref name=RHSAZ>{{cite book|title=RHS A-Z encyclopedia of garden plants|year=2008|publisher=Dorling Kindersley|location=United Kingdom|isbn=978-1405332965|pages=1136}}</ref> Under the synonym ''Philodendron scandens'' it has gained the [[Royal Horticultural Society]]'s [[Award of Garden Merit]].<ref>{{cite web|title=RHS Plant Selector - ''Philodendron scandens''|url=https://www.rhs.org.uk/Plants/59193/Philodendron-scandens/Details|access-date=6 February 2021|archive-date=19 May 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240519083238/https://www.rhs.org.uk/plants/59193/philodendron-scandens/details|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web | url = https://www.rhs.org.uk/plants/pdfs/agm-lists/agm-ornamentals.pdf | title = AGM Plants - Ornamental | date = July 2017 | page = 76 | publisher = Royal Horticultural Society | accessdate = 24 April 2018 | archive-date = 2 May 2020 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20200502143155/https://www.rhs.org.uk/plants/pdfs/agm-lists/agm-ornamentals.pdf | url-status = live }}</ref> ''P. hederaceum'' have simple leaves that are arranged in an alternate fashion, meaning single leaves are born on alternating nodes.<ref>{{cite web | url = https://www.britannica.com/plant/angiosperm/Stems#ref596636 | title = Angiosperm | publisher = Martin Huldrych Zimmermann | accessdate = 7 October 2023 | archive-date = 15 October 2023 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20231015172518/https://www.britannica.com/plant/angiosperm/Stems#ref596636 | url-status = live }}</ref>

''P. hederaceum'' can grow with its roots both in and out of the soil, deeming it a semi-epiphyte.<ref>{{cite web | url = https://anaturalcuriosity.org/philodendron-hederaceum/ | title = Philodendron Hederaceum: For people who want to know more about the heart leaf philodendron | publisher = University of Minnesota Press | accessdate = 6 October 2023 | archive-date = 14 April 2024 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20240414103059/https://anaturalcuriosity.org/philodendron-hederaceum/ | url-status = live }}</ref> Its uses include ornamental household decoration. It is a popular choice for such display due to its striking green coloration, spiraling, fast growing leaves, and convenient size. ''P.&nbsp;hederaceum'' are also easy to maintain, requiring medium sunlight and moist soil.<ref>{{cite web | url = https://homegarden.cahnr.uconn.edu/wp-content/uploads/sites/3479/2022/08/Philodendron.pdf | title = Philodendron | publisher = University of Connecticut | accessdate = 6 October 2023 | archive-date = 19 October 2023 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20231019205102/https://homegarden.cahnr.uconn.edu/wp-content/uploads/sites/3479/2022/08/Philodendron.pdf | url-status = live }}</ref> ''Philodendron hederaceum'' will eventually grow white flowers (approximately 15 years after planting). Inflorescences are rare; spike [[inflorescences]] are the most common when one does appear. They have the capacity to bear fruits as well. Philodendron fruits are typically orange, red, or white berries.<ref>{{cite web | url = https://plants.ces.ncsu.edu/plants/philodendron-hederaceum-var-hederaceum/ | title = Philodendron Hederaceum var. hederaceum | publisher = NC State University | accessdate = 4 October 2023 | archive-date = 26 February 2024 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20240226195513/https://plants.ces.ncsu.edu/plants/philodendron-hederaceum-var-hederaceum/ | url-status = dead }}</ref> Philodendron are particularly skilled at eliminating toxins such as [[formaldehyde]] from the air.<ref>{{cite web |date=2010 |title=Variation in Formaldehyde Removal Efficiency among Indoor Plant Species |url=https://greenplantsforgreenbuildings.org/wp-content/uploads/2014/09/Formaldehyde-removal-plant-species.pdf |accessdate=15 October 2023 |publisher=Hort Science |archive-date=22 June 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240622110438/https://greenplantsforgreenbuildings.org//wp-content/uploads/2014/09/Formaldehyde-removal-plant-species.pdf |url-status=live }}</ref>

==Toxicity==
Parts of the plant are known to contain [[calcium oxalate]] crystals in varying concentrations. These calcium oxalate crystals are deposited in organs such as the kidneys, and can cause cardiac-related issues in humans.<ref>{{cite web | url = https://librarysftp.whitworth.edu:2443/login?url=https://www.proquest.com/scholarly-journals/review-on-philodendron-species-plant-seeking/docview/2237019073 | title = Review on Philodendron Species-Plant Seeking for Validation of its Therapeutic Approaches | date = May 2019 | publisher = Journal of Pharmaceutical Sciences and Research | accessdate = 4 October 2023 | archive-date = 21 June 2024 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20240621090245/https://librarysftp.whitworth.edu:2443/login?url=https://www.proquest.com/scholarly-journals/review-on-philodendron-species-plant-seeking/docview/2237019073 | url-status = live }}</ref> Although the plant is known to be toxic to mice and rats, the current literature is conflicting with regards to its toxicity in cats.<ref>Der Marderosian, Ara and Giller, F. and Roia, F. (1976). ''Phytochemical and Toxicological Screening of Household Ornamental Plants Potentially Toxic to Humans''. J. Toxicol. Environ. Health. 1: 939-953.</ref><ref>Greer, M. J. (1961). ''Plant Poisonings in Cats''. Mod. Vet. Pract., 42, 62.</ref><ref name="Sarah J. Sellers, pp.92-96">Sellers, Sarah J. and King, Maralee and Aronson, Carl E. and Der Marderosian, Ara (1978). ''Toxocologic Assessment of Philodendron Oxycardium Schott (Araceae) in Domestic Cats''. Veterinary and Human Toxicology. Vol. 20, pp.&nbsp;92-96, ISSN 0145-6296</ref>

Although ''Philodendron'' species can be harmful if ingested, they also have a reputation of being a source of potential therapeutic applications for medical research.<ref>{{cite web | url = https://librarysftp.whitworth.edu:2443/login?url=https://www.proquest.com/scholarly-journals/review-on-philodendron-species-plant-seeking/docview/2237019073 | title = Review on Philodendron Species-Plant Seeking for Validation of its Therapeutic Approaches | date = May 2019 | publisher = Journal of Pharmaceutical Sciences and Research | accessdate = 4 October 2023 | archive-date = 21 June 2024 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20240621090245/https://librarysftp.whitworth.edu:2443/login?url=https://www.proquest.com/scholarly-journals/review-on-philodendron-species-plant-seeking/docview/2237019073 | url-status = live }}</ref> This reputation is due to the presence of [[terpenoids]] and [[flavonoids]], natural compounds that are of interest because of their biological abilities in acting as an antioxidant, anti-inflammatory, and disease preventative.<ref>{{cite journal | title = Potential of Terpenoids and Flavonoids from Asteraceae as Anti-Inflammatory, Antitumor, and Antiparasitic Agents | date = 12 July 2017 | publisher = National Institutes of Health | pmc = 5529648 | last1 = Sülsen | first1 = V. P. | last2 = Lizarraga | first2 = E. | last3 = Mamadalieva | first3 = N. Z. | last4 = Lago | first4 = J. H. | journal = Evidence-Based Complementary and Alternative Medicine | volume = 2017 | pages = 1–2 | doi = 10.1155/2017/6196198 | pmid = 28785291 | doi-access = free }}</ref>

According to the [[American Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Animals]], ''Philodendron hederaceum'' can cause oral irritation, a painful and swollen mouth, lips, and tongue, drooling, vomiting (except in horses), and difficulty swallowing in animals.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Heartleaf Philodendron |url=https://www.aspca.org/pet-care/animal-poison-control/toxic-and-non-toxic-plants/heartleaf-philodendron |access-date=2024-08-12 |website=ASPCA |language=en |archive-date=2017-08-27 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170827171305/https://www.aspca.org/pet-care/animal-poison-control/toxic-and-non-toxic-plants/heartleaf-philodendron |url-status=live }}</ref>

==Propagation==
Layering and cutting are two of the favored techniques when it comes to propagating ''P.&nbsp;hederaceum''. It is best to propagate during the spring and summer.<ref>{{cite journal | url = https://www.jstor.org/stable/pdf/1948590.pdf | title = The Plant Life of Cuba | date = October 1943 | publisher = Ecological Society of America | jstor = 1948590 | accessdate = 15 October 2023 | last1 = Seifriz | first1 = William | journal = Ecological Monographs | volume = 13 | issue = 4 | pages = 375–426 | doi = 10.2307/1948590 | bibcode = 1943EcoM...13..375S }}</ref> To begin propagation, locate an axillary bud and cut a branch directly beneath it. This branch should then be placed in soil or water with a small amount of activated charcoal.<ref>{{cite web | url = https://digitalcommons.unf.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1169&context=soars | title = Propagation, upkeep, and impact on mental health and classroom/workspace success of Dracaena trifasciata, Chlorophytum comosum, and Philodendron hederaceum | publisher = University of North Florida | accessdate = 15 October 2023 | archive-date = 10 July 2024 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20240710030229/https://digitalcommons.unf.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1169&context=soars | url-status = live }}</ref> Treatment includes moving the plant to a place of bright sunlight, but one should avoid direct sunlight.<ref>{{cite web | url = https://librarysftp.whitworth.edu:2443/login?url=https://www.proquest.com/scholarly-journals/review-on-philodendron-species-plant-seeking/docview/2237019073 | title = Review on Philodendron Species-Plant Seeking for Validation of its Therapeutic Approaches | date = May 2019 | publisher =  | accessdate = 4 October 2023 | archive-date = 21 June 2024 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20240621090245/https://librarysftp.whitworth.edu:2443/login?url=https://www.proquest.com/scholarly-journals/review-on-philodendron-species-plant-seeking/docview/2237019073 | url-status = live |website=Journal of Pharmaceutical Sciences and Research}}</ref>

